WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.167
-It's so great to you.

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.868
I am the biggest, biggest,
biggest, biggest fan,

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:07.100
but I want to get to know more
about you so everyone knows.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.634
And again, I don't want
to give away any spoilers,

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.033
but you were so great on "Ozark"

00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:13.267
that I hope you won every award

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:15.534
that was known to man
to give to an actor.

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:17.400
You deserve it.
You were great.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.467
-Wow, thank you.
-That was phenomenal.

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:20.667
-Thank you.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:21.968
-And I'm reading up about you.

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:24.501
And I go, wait, you got your
start on "Guiding Light"?

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:26.334
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:27.868
-What?
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:30.501
I started on "Guiding Light"
out of college, you know?

00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:32.033
And so, I went
to theater school

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.534
and I'm learning
my theater acting...

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:35.234
-Sure.
-My big -- my outdoor acting,

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:36.567
I now call it.
-Yeah.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:37.834
-And then you go on television.

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:39.567
I had never been in front
of the camera before.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:41.767
So it was like, what are you
supposed to do?

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.434
You know, and people say, like,
you do less or you do nothing.

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.334
-Because you're used to
projecting your voice on stage.

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:48.701
-Yeah, your kind of --
your voice and your body.

00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:49.901
-Theater.
-And all -- you know, yeah.

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:50.968
-So now you're on camera, yeah.

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:53.133
-So now I'm on camera,
and I'm like,

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.667
I'm not going to do anything,
you know.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:55.868
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:57.200
-I'm not going to do anything.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.400
-I'm literally
not going to do anything.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.567
And I was like, "Oh, I'm cool.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.501
Like, I bet --
I bet I look cool

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:04.033
and I bet this is, like,
good camera acting."

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.868
And because the soap, you know,
like, they --

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:08.701
they air it a month after.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.901
So you're filming
an episode a day,

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.501
and they're airing it
a month later.

00:01:12.501 --> 00:01:13.701
And that just happens
around the clock.

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:15.200
-It's work.
They go for it on soap operas.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.801
-Yeah.
-It's like, you do like 50 pages

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.100
a day or something.
-50 pages a day.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.167
You do an hour a day.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:21.434
So then a month later, I'm like,

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:23.534
"Now let me see
what my camera acting,

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:24.567
you know, let's see how this
worked out."

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:25.901
-Hello, let's James Dean.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:27.834
Let's see what happens
if James Dean enters the room.

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:31.501
-Well, James Dean looked like
he had a pole up his butt.

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:33.801
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.801 --> 00:01:35.067
I did.
-It was -- it was terrible?

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:37.601
-It was so bad. It looked
like I was scared to move.

00:01:37.601 --> 00:01:39.400
I was like...
-"Hi."

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.601
[ Laughs ]
-Oh, really. So, it's awful.

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:44.400
And you saw it, and you go,
"Oh, no, this is rough."

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.601
-It was horrible.
-Did your friends and family

00:01:46.601 --> 00:01:48.868
say, "Oh, no, dude, Tom, you got
to figure this out."

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.167
-No, I -- I was my own
harshest critic.

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.400
-You knew it.
-I knew it.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.734
-You knew it.
-But it was great, because then

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:55.968
you kind of learn --
learn as you go.

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:57.200
-You just learn to change it.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.534
And then, you ended up winning
two Emmys for it.

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:02.100
-Yeah.
-Dude, yeah.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.901
So you learned how to do it.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:06.400
You figured it out.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.167
You know, it's teacher
appreciation week,

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:09.868
and I know that you wanted
to give a shout out

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:11.501
to a certain teacher of yours.

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:13.200
The stage is yours.
The floor is yours.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.834
You can go to camera two
or camera one.

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:18.801
-Yeah, well, when I was
14-years-old in high school,

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:23.200
I had a teacher named
Steve Kasakoff, and, I guess --

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.467
I guess he really
changed my life.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:28.767
You know, he was a man that
introduced me to acting

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:31.334
and gave me my first exposure
to the whole thing.

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:33.601
And he did it with love
and passion,

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.601
and he taught me discipline.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:38.601
And I'm going to get emotional.

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:40.167
He gave me a work ethic.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:44.667
And I wouldn't be here
or have the life I have

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.000
right now without him,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.467
and I'm so grateful for that,

00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.901
and I'm so grateful
for all the people

00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:57.267
and the teachers and the adults
who take time to show

00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:01.100
an interest in kids and kind of
turn their lights on.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.968
And -- yeah, happy --
happy teacher week.

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:05.234
-Teacher appreciation.
Yeah.

00:03:05.234 --> 00:03:07.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.634
-[ Laughs ]
-Thank you for doing that.

00:03:08.634 --> 00:03:11.534
Thank you for saying that.
Love that.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:13.033
Where would we be
without our teachers?

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:15.033
We're lucky enough
to have teachers.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:18.934
I mean, he's got to be proud
of your work on "Ozark."

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:22.267
I got to be honest, you play
Ben, you know,

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:23.968
Laura Linney's brother,
you know, on the show.

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:27.934
And I'm watching, you know,
and again, no spoilers,

00:03:27.934 --> 00:03:29.667
I'm not quite to the end.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:32.033
So I don't know anything,

00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:35.067
but I do know that what I saw
was just amazing.

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:37.734
And it was just --
it was just --

00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:41.067
I was scared of you,
then I loved you.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:42.334
I rooted for you.

00:03:42.334 --> 00:03:43.434
I mean, I didn't love you,
but I rooted for you.

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:44.834
-Right, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:46.133
-And then -- then you got
romantic, so I go,

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:47.501
"Oh, this guy's a romantic."
-Yeah.

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:50.000
-I'm rooting for you guys.
I'm always rooting for love.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.033
And then -- then I didn't know

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:53.934
what was going
to happen with you,

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:56.300
and you made me so nervous,
and I haven't seen

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.334
acting like that in a long time.

00:03:58.334 --> 00:03:59.834
It was just phenomenal
what you did.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:01.734
Did you know
when you were doing it?

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:04.667
Like, "Oh, this -- I'm hitting
it out of the park here."

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:06.100
-Oh, no. No.
-Really?

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.901
-Yeah.
-Wow.

00:04:07.901 --> 00:04:09.200
-Yeah, you don't --
you don't know.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.434
Like, when it -- when it's kind
of really good,

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:14.033
you kind of don't know
really what's happening

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.367
and you forget a little bit
and you're not really sure.

00:04:16.367 --> 00:04:18.667
But, thankfully I'm surrounded
by, you know,

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.200
Laura Linney and Jason Bateman.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.133
And it's like --
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.767
"Are we okay?"
"Yeah, we're good."

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:25.100
-We're good.
-We're just --

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.634
-I mean, yeah, they're all --
they're all great actors.

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:28.467
The whole cast is amazing.
-Amazing.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:29.868
-Everyone scores, obviously.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.734
But I just saw you,
and I was like,

00:04:31.734 --> 00:04:33.000
"This guy is just..."

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.167
Not even a home run.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.501
Just knocked the cover
off the ball, buddy.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:37.300
It was amazing.
-Dude, that means so much to me.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.634
-Really, you're great.

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:40.300
And now I want to talk about
"Outer Range."

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.834
Okay, this is on Prime Video.

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:44.033
Here, this is -- set this up,
because this is a great --

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:45.267
this is a great plot.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:48.033
-Yeah. So, I mean,
it's a lot of things.

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:50.801
So, you kind of have this --
it's a modern day

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:56.033
Western family drama, mystery

00:04:56.033 --> 00:05:00.801
with a metaphysical element
and crazy neighbors.

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:03.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:05.133
-If you can't relate to that,
then I mean that's -- exactly.

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:06.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:08.167
No, but it's --
so it's basically -- yeah,

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.434
you're on horseback a lot.

00:05:10.434 --> 00:05:12.000
You're herding cattle.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.334
There's --
your wife goes missing.

00:05:15.334 --> 00:05:16.601
-She's missing, yeah.

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:19.067
When the show starts, she's been
missing for nine months.

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:22.634
And my character, Perry Abbott,
the son of Royal Abbott,

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:25.300
who is played by Josh Brolin,
is sort of --

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.033
sort of broken,
sort of -- kind of lost.

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:28.133
His wife's missing,

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:30.067
and he doesn't know why
or what happened.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:31.901
And they don't know
if she's alive or dead,

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:35.133
and he's sort of in that limbo
when we meet him.

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:37.934
-Yeah.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:40.667
Here is Tom Pelphrey and
Josh Brolin in "Outer Range."

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.267
Take a look at this.

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:44.334
-She said that Rebecca's
not gone.

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:48.200
♪♪

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.267
That nothing's ever really gone.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:54.501
♪♪

00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:55.934
That there's a plan.

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:59.334
♪♪

00:05:59.334 --> 00:06:01.734
-What do you mean?

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:02.834
[ Chuckles ]

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:11.033
♪♪

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:13.801
And saved you.

00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:17.267
-No, this isn't that.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:18.400
-It brought you here.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:19.501
-Perry.

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:22.234
♪♪

00:06:22.234 --> 00:06:24.367
-Dad.

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:25.367
-Son, listen to me.

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:27.067
-She's right.

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:30.067
-Oh, Tom Pelphrey, everyone.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:33.167
Season one of "Outer Range" is
available now on Prime Video.

